# AnythingShort — Cursor Project Rules

## Stack Lock
- Next.js 15, App Router ONLY. No pages/ directory, no getServerSideProps.
- TypeScript strict mode is non-negotiable. tsconfig must have "strict": true,
  "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true, "noImplicitAny": true.
- Prisma as the ORM. All DB access goes through a single shared Prisma client
  instance (lib/prisma.ts), never `new PrismaClient()` inline anywhere else.
- Tailwind + Shadcn UI for all components. No inline style objects unless
  dynamically computed (e.g. chart colors).
- Server Components by default. Only add "use client" when the component
  needs interactivity, hooks, or browser APIs. Justify every "use client"
  with a one-line comment above it.

## Architecture Rules
- Route Handlers (app/api/**/route.ts) contain NO business logic. They:
  1. Parse/validate input (Zod)
  2. Call a service function from lib/services/*
  3. Return a typed NextResponse
  All actual logic lives in lib/services/. This keeps logic testable and
  route handlers thin.
- Every external I/O call (Prisma, Redis, Auth) must be wrapped in try/catch
  with typed error responses. Never let an unhandled promise rejection reach
  the client as a raw 500 with a stack trace.
- No hardcoded secrets, API keys, DB URLs, or magic tier limits anywhere in
  source. Everything sensitive or configurable goes in `.env.local` and is
  accessed through a validated `lib/env.ts` (use Zod to parse
  process.env at boot, fail fast if a required var is missing).
- Zero `any` types. If a third-party lib lacks types, write a minimal .d.ts
  in types/.

## File/Folder Conventions
app/
  (marketing)/           -> public landing pages
  (dashboard)/            -> authenticated app, wrapped in dashboard layout
  api/                     -> route handlers only, thin
  [slug]/route.ts          -> the redirection engine (root-level catch route)
lib/
  services/                -> business logic (links.ts, analytics.ts, auth.ts)
  redis.ts                 -> single Upstash client instance
  prisma.ts                -> single Prisma client instance
  ratelimit.ts              -> Upstash ratelimit instances
  base62.ts                -> encode/decode utils
  env.ts                    -> validated env access
components/
  ui/                       -> shadcn primitives (do not hand-edit generated ones)
  dashboard/                -> feature components
types/

## Error Handling
- Use a consistent ApiError class (lib/errors.ts) with `.status` and
  `.message`. Route handlers catch it and map to NextResponse.json with the
  right status code. Never leak Prisma/Postgres error internals to the client.

## Naming
- DB models: PascalCase singular (User, Link, Click).
- DB columns: camelCase in Prisma schema (Prisma maps to snake_case in
  Postgres via @map where needed).
- API routes: kebab-case.

## Performance Rules
- Any operation on the hot redirect path ([slug]/route.ts) must be
  benchmarked mentally against "does this block the redirect?" If yes, move
  it to a fire-and-forget async call or a queue. The redirect response must
  return before analytics writes complete.
- Never do a Postgres read in the redirect path if a Redis cache hit is
  possible. Cache-aside pattern: check Redis -> miss -> query Postgres ->
  populate Redis -> return.

## Testing/Verification Discipline
- After generating any phase, run `npx tsc --noEmit` and fix all errors
  before proceeding. Do not let type errors accumulate across phases.
- Every new API route needs a matching entry in a manually maintained
  API.md describing method, path, body shape, response shape, and auth
  requirement.

## What NOT to do
- Do not install a UI kit other than Shadcn.
- Do not use client-side fetching (SWR/React Query) for data that can be
  fetched server-side in a Server Component, unless it needs live polling
  (e.g. analytics auto-refresh).
- Do not write raw SQL unless Prisma genuinely cannot express the query
  (e.g. complex aggregation) — and if you do, isolate it in
  lib/services/rawQueries.ts with a comment explaining why.